WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:02.684
Albert Schweitzer was a man of many talents.

2
00:00:02.685 --> 00:00:11.275
From his achievements as an organist and composer to his work as a pastor, philosopher, and physician, he had a deep reverence for everyone he met.

3
00:00:11.276 --> 00:00:17.729
Reverence is defined as deep respect and admiration, and Albert Schweitzer took this meaning seriously.

4
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.995
He believed that all people should be guided by a philosophy he called a "reverence for life."

5
00:00:22.996 --> 00:00:32.394
In his book The Philosophy of Civilization , Schweitzer wrote, "It is good to maintain and encourage life; it is bad to destroy life or obstruct

6
00:00:32.395 --> 00:00:36.446
it." This simple idea became the foundation of everything he

7
00:00:36.447 --> 00:00:46.098
accomplished. Albert Schweitzer was born on January 14, 1875, in a country village in Alsace, then a part of Germany but now a part of

8
00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:53.970
France. His grandfather and great-grandfather had been schoolmasters and organists in the region, and his father was a Protestant pastor and teacher.

9
00:00:53.971 --> 00:01:01.687
When Schweitzer was six months old, his father became the pastor of a local church in G&uuml;nsbach, France, and moved his family there.

10
00:01:01.688 --> 00:01:09.623
In G&uuml;nsbach, the boy grew up happily with his three sisters and one brother, and from an early age, Schweitzer was interested in music.

11
00:01:09.624 --> 00:01:12.976
His father started teaching him piano when he was five years old.

12
00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:23.026
Later Schweitzer would write in his autobiography, Out of My Life and Thought , "At eight, when my legs were hardly long enough to reach the pedals, I began to play the

13
00:01:23.027 --> 00:01:29.452
organ." In G&uuml;nsbach, there was only one church, so both Catholics and Protestants had to share the building to

14
00:01:29.453 --> 00:01:37.945
worship. This closeness fostered a religious acceptance and tolerance of others in the town and nurtured a belief in human kindness and charity in young

15
00:01:37.946 --> 00:01:43.731
Albert. This influence, plus his father's religious teachings, would become a fundamental part of his

16
00:01:43.732 --> 00:01:48.545
character. Although Schweitzer was an inquisitive boy, his academic performance was

17
00:01:48.546 --> 00:01:53.097
disappointing. "I did not cut a brilliant figure," admitted Schweitzer of his early school

18
00:01:53.098 --> 00:02:03.814
years. But being obviously talented in music and equally interested in religion, Schweitzer blossomed as he grew older and soon found himself struggling with a choice on which direction his life should

19
00:02:03.815 --> 00:02:09.765
take. Eventually he decided that the two interests were not mutually exclusive and he would pursue

20
00:02:09.766 --> 00:02:21.754
both. While continuing to study music privately, Schweitzer enrolled at the University of Strasbourg in 1893 to pursue both theology and philosophy, eventually receiving a doctorate in both

21
00:02:21.755 --> 00:02:29.572
disciplines. By 1899, Schweitzer had become a pastor at Saint Nicholas Church in Strasbourg, but he continued to play

22
00:02:29.573 --> 00:02:38.287
music. He became well-known as an organist with a unique sense of interpreting the compositions of Johann Sebastian Bach, playing concerts across Europe.

23
00:02:38.288 --> 00:02:48.211
In 1905 Schweitzer wrote a biography of Bach, J.S. Bach: The Musician-Poet , which established Schweitzer as a music scholar as well as a

24
00:02:48.212 --> 00:03:03.704
philosopher. Just a year later, Schweitzer published his most important book, The Quest of the Historical Jesus , which gained him much scholarly recognition for its historical presentation of the life of Jesus and influenced theological thought throughout the

25
00:03:03.705 --> 00:03:10.973
twentieth century. Even though he had established himself as an author, a pastor, and a talented musician, Schweitzer was

26
00:03:10.974 --> 00:03:20.078
not satisfied. Inspired by a magazine article about the desperate need for doctors in the French colonies in Africa, Schweitzer decided to devote himself to helping the

27
00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:26.687
people there. So Albert Schweitzer enrolled in medical school and dedicated the next eight years to the study

28
00:03:26.688 --> 00:03:38.017
of medicine. In 1913 the 38-year-old Schweitzer, by then an expert in tropical medicine, was ready to travel to the French Congo (in an area that is now Gabon) to begin

29
00:03:38.018 --> 00:03:44.882
his mission. But one detail remained: Schweitzer had been assisted in his studies by a young nurse named

30
00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:54.113
H&eacute;l&egrave;ne Bresslau. They planned to operate the hospital in Africa together, but the couple felt it would not be proper for a woman to accompany a single man into the

31
00:03:54.114 --> 00:03:58.833
African jungle. So the pair married and left for Africa in March

32
00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:04.443
of 1913. Upon their arrival in the Congo, the Schweitzers started their hospital in an old

33
00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:10.815
chicken coop. Later, they added a metal building with a palm-leaf roof, which became his operating room

34
00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:19.688
and office. Africans in this region did not have access to the latest medical treatment, and Schweitzer began caring for people with malaria, leprosy, and

35
00:04:19.689 --> 00:04:27.418
other ailments. He quickly noticed that many medical conditions easily treatable in a European hospital could be a death sentence for people in this remote

36
00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:30.192
region. Schweitzer's presence saved many

37
00:04:30.193 --> 00:04:35.921
lives. However, just as Schweitzer's hospital began to thrive, his work was interrupted by World War

38
00:04:35.922 --> 00:04:43.356
I. Schweitzer and his wife, both German citizens, were sent to a French prison camp until the end of the war in

39
00:04:43.357 --> 00:04:54.616
1918. After being released, they returned to Alsace, and Schweitzer spent the next six years preaching, writing, giving lectures, performing, and expanding his knowledge of

40
00:04:54.617 --> 00:05:03.052
medicine. In 1924 Schweitzer returned to the Congo, and except for short speaking tours, remained in Africa for the rest of his

41
00:05:03.053 --> 00:05:10.660
life. Schweitzer had finally been able to grasp the ethical concept he had been searching for since he was a young man, and he decided to write about

42
00:05:10.661 --> 00:05:21.081
it. In his autobiography, Schweitzer wrote how he was finally able to put a name to this concept when he was traveling up an African river by steamship to reach a sick

43
00:05:21.082 --> 00:05:24.859
missionary. The insight struck him as he sat on the deck, lost in

44
00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:34.302
thought. "We were making our way through a herd of hippopotamuses, [when it] flashed upon my mind, unforeseen and unsought, the phrase, 'Reverence for

45
00:05:34.303 --> 00:05:41.246
Life.'" This idea would become his motto to perfectly illustrate the way all people should respond to the world around

46
00:05:41.247 --> 00:05:47.458
them. Schweitzer's "reverence for life" expressed the idea that humans were not created to control or subdue

47
00:05:47.459 --> 00:05:53.044
nature. Respect for life, in whatever form, should take priority over any human

48
00:05:53.045 --> 00:06:00.059
intentions. He believed everyone had an obligation to do as little harm as possible while providing aid to others whenever

49
00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.737
necessary. Schweitzer's views were in direct conflict with the colonial worldview of European nations of the

50
00:06:05.738 --> 00:06:17.938
time. Feeling superior to native populations, Europeans eagerly expanded their influence over non-European territories, but Schweitzer believed that they did not have the right to impose their will upon others.

51
00:06:17.939 --> 00:06:26.477
Schweitzer's work and life was a model for many, and in 1952, he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for his work in Africa.

52
00:06:26.478 --> 00:06:37.918
Albert Schweitzer spoke out for peace and against the horrors of nuclear weapons, delivering major speeches against weapons of mass destruction between 1957 and 1958.

53
00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:46.918
When the United States, Britain, and the Soviet Union signed the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty in 1963, Schweitzer saw it as a sign of hope.

54
00:06:46.919 --> 00:06:55.959
Albert Schweitzer lived out his final years in his beloved jungle hospital, and on September 4, 1965, he died at the age of ninety.

55
00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:03.515
He was buried next to his wife H&eacute;l&egrave;ne, on the bank of the river that leads to his hospital, which continues to operate to this day.

